.TH mnnn-sync 1  "mnnn-sync 2.10.0" 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
mnnn\-sync \- Sync: every node linked to your user keeps all of your data; conflicts are kept, never lost; bars on request
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmnnn sync\fR [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sync: every node linked to your user keeps all of your data; conflicts are kept, never lost; bars on request
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Muninn home directory (default ~/.muninn)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_HOME\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Database file (default <home>/storage/muninn.database)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR
Never prompt; missing values are errors
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_NON_INTERACTIVE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
More output (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing but errors and requested data
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_QUIET\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to every confirmation
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_YES\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR \fI<THEME_MODE>\fR
Override the theme mode for this run
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
light
.IP \(bu 2
dark
.IP \(bu 2
system
.RE
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_THEME_MODE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH NOTES
.PP
A running node pulls what changed from each of your linked nodes about once a minute, over
the key exchange and sealed with a key only your nodes hold. Rows changed on both nodes
keep the newer one; the other is saved as a conflict (`mnnn sync conflicts`) and can be put
back with `mnnn sync resolve ID \-\-take\-other`. A delete never wins over an edit. Bars are
not synced on their own: `mnnn sync bars` fetches them. Revoked nodes are not answered.
See `mnnn help sync`.
.PP
Examples:
  mnnn sync status
  mnnn sync now
  mnnn sync conflicts
  mnnn sync show 3f2a
  mnnn sync resolve 3f2a \-\-take\-other
  mnnn sync bars \-\-source binance \-\-symbol BTCUSDT

.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-status(1)
Your linked nodes: when each last synced, what it brought and open conflicts
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-now(1)
Pull from your linked nodes now (all, or one by label or peer id)
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-conflicts(1)
Rows changed on two nodes at once: which one was kept, and the other
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-show(1)
Both sides of one conflict
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-resolve(1)
Settle a conflict: keep what is there, or put the other row back (\-\-take\-other)
.TP
mnnn\-sync\-bars(1)
Fetch bars from a linked node (all series, or some)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
